Problems found
==============

1. “Security-by-header” (critical)  
   • Anybody that can reach the route can add  
     `X-Admin: true` to the request and instantly become an “admin”.  
   • Nothing proves that the request really comes from an authenticated user, let alone an
     administrator.

2. No cryptographic proof / no authentication context  
   • There is no session, JWT or API-key verification before the role check.  
   • Because authentication is missing, **authorisation is meaningless**.

3. Possible logic bug – missing return after next()  
   • If the middleware is later refactored and code is appended after `next()`, it will run for
     the same request and may try to send another response, producing the classic  
     “Can’t set headers after they are sent” error.

4. Header value comparison is brittle  
   • `== 'true'` is case- and white-space-sensitive.  
   • Duplicate headers (e.g. two `X-Admin` lines) are collapsed by Node into the string  
     `"true, true"` – the comparison will then fail unexpectedly.

5. No type validation  
   • `req.headers['x-admin']` can be `undefined`, a string or an array (when multiple headers
     are sent).  The code assumes string.

6. Error message leaks implementation detail  
   • Returning “Not admin” tells the attacker something about the access control logic.

7. Non-standard naming  
   • Custom headers should be prefixed with `X-` only for historical reasons; the current
     recommendation (RFC 6648) is to avoid the `X-` prefix when you control both ends.

How to fix it
=============

1. Do not decide admin status from a raw header  
   • Authenticate the request first (session cookie, OAuth token, JWT, mTLS, etc.).  
   • Attach the user object to `req` (e.g. `req.user`) in the authentication middleware.  
   • Perform the role check against that trusted object.

Example with a previously verified JWT:

```js
// auth.js (run **before** checkAdmin)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authenticate(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing token' });

  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = payload;               // { id, role, ... }
    return next();
  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
  }
}
```

```js
// checkAdmin.js
function checkAdmin(req, res, next) {
  if (req.user?.role === 'admin') {
    return next();                    // <— return added
  }
  return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
}
```

2. Defensive coding of the middleware if you must keep the header

```js
function checkAdmin(req, res, next) {
  const adminHeader = req.headers['x-admin'];

  const isAdmin = Array.isArray(adminHeader)
    ? adminHeader.some(h => h.trim().toLowerCase() === 'true')
    : typeof adminHeader === 'string' &&
      adminHeader.trim().toLowerCase() === 'true';

  if (isAdmin) return next();
  return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
}
```

3. General best practices
   • Always `return` after sending a response or calling `next()` to avoid double sends.  
   • Put authentication and authorisation checks in separate, reusable middlewares.  
   • Use constants or an enum for role names to prevent typos.  
   • Log denial events server-side (but do not expose details to the client).  
   • Add rate limiting and CSRF protection where appropriate.

By replacing the spoofable header check with proper authentication + role verification and tightening the control flow, you remove the trivial privilege-escalation bug and make the middleware safe for production use.
